  "hub": "a function in the interlinked code that takes register inputs and is called from two or more other interlinked functions. Hubs are counted, never named.",
  "hubs_waited_on": "the number of distinct hubs a group's functions are or call directly. A hub can serve several groups or areas, so these counts overlap.",
  "core": "the tightly meshed centre of the interlinked code, where no single hub cuts a piece off; the rest hangs off it through one hub each.",
  "communities": "the interlinked code split into groups of densely interlinked functions by a standard community-detection method (Louvain); each is named after the feature area that holds most of its bytes, with letters when an area has several.",
  "heuristic": "areas, kinds, readiness and communities are computed mechanically but rest on heuristics (area roll-up, core/fringe split, plateau classes, transcribed holds); treat them as a map, not a ledger."
 }
}
